      "stageRecord": "The completed clause adopts the standard CP treatment of Estonian matrix verb-second order. First, a declarative C is introduced above IP, establishing an unpronounced clause-typing head with a finite-head requirement and a preverbal specifier position. Second, the complex I head established in the preceding stage raises to C, leaving a silent I trace; because V had already raised to I, the finite verb reaches C through the intervening head rather than skipping I. Third, Mari moves from the previously represented Spec-IP position to Spec-CP, leaving a second NP trace. This A′ step supplies the subject as the initial constituent in the V2 clause; it does not force a contrastive-topic interpretation. The resulting subject chain connects Spec-CP, Spec-IP, and Spec-VP, preserving nominative licensing at the intermediate position and the Agent role at the lowest position. The finite head chain connects C, I, and V, preserving the lexical predicate's relation to the partitive object. Finally, Mari is an R-expression with no coindexed c-commanding antecedent, so Principle C is satisfied. There are no pronouns or anaphors requiring a separate Principle A or B dependency. The only pronounced terminals, in tree order, are Mari, loeb, uut, and ajalehte. The original sentence's final period is not a supplied input token and is not represented as a syntactic terminal.",
